# Yosys synthesis script for the whole SoC (Phase 7 sign-off), targeting
# the Nangate45 open-cell library.
#
# The three plain memory arrays (boot_rom's 64KB ROM, sram's 128KB RAM,
# dma_ram's 8KB scratch buffer) are swapped for blackbox stand-ins
# (mem_blackboxes.v) instead of their real `reg [31:0] mem [...]` bodies --
# in a real ASIC flow these would be hardened SRAM macros, not synthesized
# flip-flops, and letting Yosys actually flatten a 32K-word array into
# 1M+ flip-flops would be both unrepresentative and impractically slow.
# Everything else -- PicoRV32, the hand-written crossbar, every
# peripheral's control/datapath logic, the AES core + CBC/CTR chaining,
# and the DMA engine -- is synthesized for real. This is what makes the
# Fmax number in docs/verification/performance.md a genuine logic-critical-path result,
# not a memory-dominated one.
#
# This is a template: @NANGATE45_LIB@/@NANGATE45_SLOW_LIB@ get substituted
# with $NANGATE45_LIB/$NANGATE45_SLOW_LIB by run_synth.sh (Yosys .ys scripts
# have no native env-var expansion).
#
# Run from blocks/soc_top/syn/ (requires NANGATE45_LIB and NANGATE45_SLOW_LIB
# set, see TOOLCHAIN.md):
#   ./run_synth.sh
read_verilog -I../../../rtl/include -I../rtl \
    mem_blackboxes.v \
    ../rtl/picorv32.v \
    ../rtl/axi_lite_xbar.v \
    ../rtl/timer.v \
    ../rtl/watchdog.v \
    ../rtl/uart.v \
    ../rtl/i2c_master.v \
    ../rtl/spi_master.v \
    ../rtl/aes_key_expand.v \
    ../rtl/aes_core.v \
    ../rtl/aes_chain.v \
    ../rtl/aes.v \
    ../rtl/dma_engine.v \
    ../rtl/jtag_tap.v \
    ../rtl/jtag_dtm.v \
    ../rtl/jtag_axi_bridge.v \
    ../rtl/soc_top.v

hierarchy -check -top soc_top
synth -top soc_top

# Gate sizing (the `buffer`/`upsize`/`dnsize` passes in abc's default
# script) only activates when a -constr file is given -- without one,
# `abc -liberty ...` alone only did area-focused technology mapping and
# never adjusted drive strength for high-fanout nets. That's why the
# active-low reset retrofit's Fmax regression (91.2->67.5MHz typical
# corner, docs/verification/performance.md section 1) went unnoticed by re-running abc
# with different `-D` delay targets alone: `-D` without `-constr` is a
# no-op in this design (confirmed empirically -- identical netlist with
# or without it). Adding -constr (constr.txt: an assumed BUF_X1 driving
# cell / 10fF load, standard placeholder values -- what matters is that a
# -constr file is present at all, not these specific numbers) recovered
# the lost timing and then some (critical path 14.821ns->2.881ns typical
# corner). See docs/verification/performance.md sections 1 and 7 for the full story.
#
# Also tried and REJECTED: flattening the hierarchy first (`synth -top
# soc_top -flatten`) so abc would see one global netlist instead of
# per-submodule scopes. That made things worse (14.821ns->38.364ns) --
# abc's heuristics degrade on a single ~127k-cell flat netlist compared
# to running per-submodule, so the existing non-flattened hierarchy is
# kept deliberately.
#
# Mapped against the SLOW corner library here (not the typical-corner
# @NANGATE45_LIB@ used everywhere else in this file/for the `stat` report
# below) so abc's sizing decisions are biased toward the worst-case PVT
# corner -- Yosys/abc has no true MMMC (multi-mode multi-corner)
# optimization, so this is the closest approximation to "size for the
# corner that actually has to meet setup" available in this toolchain.
dfflibmap -liberty @NANGATE45_SLOW_LIB@
abc -liberty @NANGATE45_SLOW_LIB@ -constr constr.txt -D 10000

clean
write_verilog soc_top_out.v
write_json soc_top_out.json

stat -liberty @NANGATE45_LIB@
